---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="fever-temperature-converter">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="fever-temperature-converter" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The thresholds, in both scales</h2>
    <p>
      A thermometer reading is useful only against a threshold, and health services publish thresholds in
      the scale they use. This table shows both.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Reading</th><th scope="col">&deg;C</th><th scope="col">&deg;F</th><th scope="col">Usual description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hypothermia</td><td>below 35.0</td><td>below 95.0</td><td>Medical emergency</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Normal range</td><td>36.1&ndash;37.5</td><td>97.0&ndash;99.5</td><td>Varies across the day</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Raised / low-grade</td><td>37.5&ndash;38.0</td><td>99.5&ndash;100.4</td><td>Above normal, below the fever line</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fever</td><td>38.0 and above</td><td>100.4 and above</td><td>The standard clinical threshold</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High fever</td><td>39.0 and above</td><td>102.2 and above</td><td>Get medical advice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Very high fever</td><td>40.0 and above</td><td>104.0 and above</td><td>Urgent assessment</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The 38.0/100.4 pair is not a rounding coincidence: 38 &deg;C is exactly 100.4 &deg;F, so both numbers
      appear in guidance worldwide.
    </p>

    <h2>&quot;Normal&quot; is a range, not 37</h2>
    <p>
      The 37 &deg;C figure comes from the 1868 study by Carl Wunderlich of about 25,000 patients, and the
      well-known 98.6 &deg;F is that round Celsius number converted. Modern studies find a lower average,
      about 36.6 &deg;C, and a real daily rhythm: body temperature is lowest in the early morning and highest
      in the late afternoon or early evening, a change of half a degree Celsius or more.
    </p>
    <p>
      So one reading shows one moment. 37.4 &deg;C at 6 pm and 37.4 &deg;C at 4 am are quite different
      observations.
    </p>

    <h2>Where you measure changes the number</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Rectal: the reference standard, usually 0.3&ndash;0.6 &deg;C above oral</li>
      <li>Tympanic (ear): close to rectal when placed correctly, easily wrong when not</li>
      <li>Oral: the basis of most published thresholds; affected by drinking and breathing through the mouth</li>
      <li>Temporal (forehead): convenient, sensitive to sweat, draughts and recent sun exposure</li>
      <li>Axillary (armpit): usually 0.3&ndash;0.6 &deg;C below oral, and the least reliable</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Compare like with like. A trend measured the same way at the same time of day tells you much more
      than one number from a different site.
    </p>

    <h2>When the number is not the point</h2>
    <p>
      The height of a fever does not show reliably how serious an illness is. These signs matter more than
      the reading: any fever in an infant under three months; a stiff neck, a rash that does not fade under
      pressure, difficulty breathing, confusion or unusual drowsiness; a fever that lasts more than three
      days; or dehydration. Each of these needs medical attention, whatever the thermometer says. This page
      converts units and lists published thresholds; it does not diagnose.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing here is recorded</h2>
    <p>
      The fever temperature converter is arithmetic in the page. There is no server, no cookies and no
      analytics. To verify, turn off your network connection: the tool works the same way.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
